[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 6, 2017, titled "A Look at the Tintry
[00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.960]   S1." Every now and then, I take a dive into an S1 to see what clears the current, ever-changing
[00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.920]   bar for going public. After a somewhat rocky IPO process, Tintry went public June 30 after
[00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.560]   cutting the IPO offering price and has traded flat thus far since then. Let's read an excerpt
[00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.800]   from this business insider story before taking a look at the numbers. With the performance of this
[00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.200]   IPO, the company is now valued at about about $231 million, based on $750 a share and its
[00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.160]   roughly 31 million outstanding shares, if the IPO's bankers don't buy their optional, additional,
[00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.480]   roughly 1.3 million shares. In other words, this IPO killed a good $570 million of the company's
[00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.720]   value. In other words, Tintry looks like a down-round IPO of Last Resort, something that frankly almost
[00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.360]   never happened before the recent mid-slash late-stage private valuation bubble of the past four years.
[00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:01.440]   Let's look at some numbers. Off-note, $125 million in FY2017 revenue. They have scale,
[00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.680]   but this is not a SaaS company so the revenue is mostly non-recurring, making it easier to get to
[00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.240]   grow quickly and making the revenue is worth less because only the support/maintenance component of
[00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.920]   it renews each year. 45% Yo-Yi total revenue growth. On the low side, especially given that
[00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.040]   they have a traditional license/maintenance model and recognize revenue on shipment. 65% gross
[00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.680]   margins, low, but they do seem to sell flash memory hardware as part of their storage solutions.
[00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.800]   87% of revenue spent on S, and M High, again particularly for a non-SaaS company. 43% of
[00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.320]   revenue spent on R&D High, but usually seen as a good ding if you view the R&D money as well spent.
[00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.440]   81% operating margins, low, particularly for a non-SaaS company.
